## ABSTRACT
We have proposed a dual quenching electrochemiluminescence (ECL) strategy which based on \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) as chromophores caged in 3D Zn-oxalate metal-organic frameworks \((\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs) for ultrasensitive detection of amyloid-\(\beta\) (A\(\beta\)). The three-dimensional chromophore connectivity in Zn-oxalate MOFs provided a network for rapid excited state energy transfer migration among \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) units which shielded the chromophores from solvent molecules and led to a high energy Ru emission efficiency. In addition, we found that both Au nanoparticles and NiFe-based nanocube MOFs could contribute to the reduction of the ECL intensity of chromophore. And the ECL emission spectra of 3D \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs overlapped appropriately with the ultraviolet visible (UV-vis) absorption spectra of Au@NiFe-MOFs composites, which could trigger the resonance energy transfer (RET) behavior between \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs (donor) and Au@NiFe-MOFs (acceptor), achieving the dual quenching effect of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) encapsulated in 3D Zn-oxalate MOFs and significantly boosting the sensitivity of the A\(\beta\)-detection immunosensor. In order to examine the clinical practicability, we have applied it to verify the content of A\(\beta\) solution ranging from \(100\;\mathrm{fg\;mL^{-1}}\) to \(50\;\mathrm{ng\;mL^{-1}}\) and obtained the calibration cure with high correlation coefficient, along with the low limit of detection of \(13.8\;\mathrm{fg\;mL^{-1}}\). Above all, this work demonstrated an approach of constructing dual quenching effect ECL immunosensors in whole 3D MOFs system and its application in ECL detection methodology.
Keywords: Dual quenching electrochemiluminescence; \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\); 3D Zn-oxalate
## metal-organic frameworks; Au@NiFe nanocube; Amyloid-\(\beta\) detection
## INTRODUCTION
Alzheimer's disease (AD) as a progressive irreversible neurological disorder, which could cause memory loss, behavior disability, and even death, has extremely threatened human health and reduced the life quality of many middle-aged and old people seriously nowadays.\(^{1,2}\) On the basis of AD study, the aggregation of amyloid-\(\beta\) (A\(\beta\)) was testified to be the pathogenesis of AD due to its extremely strong neurotoxicity.\(^{3,4}\) Therefore, A\(\beta\), which is a 39-43 amino acid peptide fragment deriving from amyloid precursor protein, was used as one of the main diagnostic biomarkers and therapeutic targets of AD.\(^{3,5,6}\) Hence, it was very meaningful to explore various efficient methods to determine the concentration of A\(\beta\) at the early stages of AD accurately and sensitively.
Specifically, electrochemiluminescence (ECL) immunosensor as an ECL immune-modified platform has drawn tremendous attention gradually due to its low background interference, simplified instrument operation, and high sensitivity, etc.\(^{7-9}\) According to the previously reported literatures, multitudinous materials were used in the ECL strategies to determinate A\(\beta\) such as ceria doped zinc oxide nanoflowers, functionalized-luminol, and silver nanoclusters/titanium oxide nanomaterials.\(^{5,7}\) And among various ECL researches, \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) was one of the most frequently-used luminophor in the ECL system on account of its stable luminous efficiency, less dosage, and pellucid luminescence mechanism.\(^{8,10,11}\) However, it was difficult to apply \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) to the solid-state sensing platform directly because of its excellent water solubility.\(^{12}\) And many methods about how to immobilize \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) in the water
environment have been explored by the researchers.\(^{13,14}\) The good news was that using MOFs materials to encapsulate \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) has become into focus due to its porosity, adjustable aperture, larger specific surface area, and homogeneous appearance.\(^{15,16}\)
In a variety of MOFs materials, the three-dimensional (3D) oxalate-MOFs has been applied increasingly to load \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) in the ECL immunosensors.\(^{17,18}\) 3D oxalate-MOFs microcrystals with chromophoric building blocks possess large enough dimensions to absorb a high fraction of light and yet micro enough for the excited states to be delivered by energy transfer processes to a redox reaction center at the MOFs/solution interface.\(^{15}\) In this work, the \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) cations were entrapped in anionic three-dimensional Zn-oxalate MOFs possessing cage structures with the purposes of increasing the dimensionality of energy transfer pathways while maintaining the electronic structure of the \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) chromophore unperturbed.\(^{19}\) And the electronic structures and energy of the \(\mathrm{D}_{3}\) symmetric \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) was much more orderly compared with those previously reported photoactive MOFs, which made the \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) exhibit long-lived luminescence lifetime and high quantum yield.\(^{20,21}\)
Furthermore, since the resonance energy transfer (RET) behavior could occur between Au@NiFe MOFs (acceptor) and \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs (donor), the Au@NiFe MOFs with the nanocubic morphology were applied as the ECL quencher of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) encapsulated in Zn-oxalate MOFs in the proposed immunosensor. Numerous studies have confirmed that the synergy of nickel and iron could modify electronic structure and improve the electrical conductivity.\(^{22,23}\) The unique porous structure of NiFe-based MOFs could also provide more active sites and shorten the
mass transport distance effectively.\(^{23-25}\) It was worth mentioning that the UV-vis absorption spectrum of NiFe-based MOFs and Au@NiFe MOFs all overlapped with the ECL emission spectra of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs, respectively. Thus, both NiFe-based MOFs and Au@NiFe MOFs could quench the luminous efficiency of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs.\(^{26,27}\) Nevertheless, due that Au nanoparticles have an absorption peak at \(550\,\textrm{nm}\), the UV-vis absorption spectrum of Au@NiFe MOFs overlapped more with the ECL emission spectrum of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs compared with NiFe-based MOFs. Thereby, the dual quenching effect of ECL efficiency of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate complex could be achieved and the sensitivity of the proposed immunosensor could be significantly improved. In this way, a dual quenching ECL immunosensor which was based on \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs as luminophor and Au@NiFe-MOFs as the dual-quencher has been constructed for the ultrasensitive detection of \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\). And this also could provide a novel approach to determine the protein toxins in the clinical medicine.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Preparation of \(\mathbf{Ru}(\mathbf{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathbf{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs. The synthesis of Zn-oxalate MOFs was based on previous report \({}^{28}\) with a little improvement. Typically, \(15\,\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(100\,\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) were added into \(45\,\mathrm{mL}\) mixture of DMF and water (2:1) with \(300\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of oxalic acid (\(0.75\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}\) in DMF) and \(3\,\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathrm{HCl}\) (\(3\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}\)). After ultrasonic vibrating for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\), the mixture was transferred to the round-bottom flask and reflux at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). The obtained orange-red precipitate was washed with DMF, ethanol and ultrapure water two times respectively and dried in
vacuum at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight.
Preparation of the Monomeric, oligomeric, and fibrous \(\mathbf{A}\upbeta\). The \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\) monomers, oligomers \((\mathrm{A}\upbeta\mathrm{O})\), and fibrils \((\mathrm{A}\upbeta\mathrm{F})\) were prepared according to previous literatures.\(^{29-33}\) Briefly, lyophilized \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\) peptide was dissolved in 1,1,1,3,3,3-hexafluoro-2-propanol and sonicated for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) to form the monomers. Afterwards, the resulting products were reconstituted in NaOH solution, diluted by PBS solution \((\mathrm{pH}\,7.4)\), and incubated for \(16\,\mathrm{h}\) at room temperature to form \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\mathrm{O}\). Similarly, the same process as above and incubated for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to form \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\mathrm{F}\). A centrifugation step was needed at \(14000\ \mathrm{r}\) for \(15\ \mathrm{min}\) to remove any insoluble aggregates.
Preparation of \(\mathbf{Ru}(\mathbf{bpy})_{2}{}^{2+}/\mathbf{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs/\(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{b}_{1}\) Bioconjugate. The synthetic process of Zn-oxalate MOFs/\(\mathrm{A}\mathrm{b}_{1}\) bioconjugate was shown in Scheme 1A. Primarily, in order to activate the carboxyl on the surface of the Zn-oxalate MOFs, \(2\ \mathrm{mL}\) of \(1\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) Zn-oxalate MOFs which dispersed in PBS solution \((\mathrm{pH}\,7.4)\) was mixed with EDC \((20\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and NHS \((10\ \mathrm{mmol})\) for \(2\ \mathrm{h}\) at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). After that, \(500\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) antibody-\(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\ (\mathrm{A}\mathrm{b}_{1})\) was added into the above mixture and continued stirring for another \(12\ \mathrm{h}\) to bind on the surface of Zn-oxalate MOFs via the amide reaction. The generated Zn-oxalate MOFs/\(\mathrm{A}\mathrm{b}_{1}\) bioconjugate was collected by centrifugation and washed with PBS solution \((\mathrm{pH}\,7.4)\) for once, ultimately dispersed in \(1\ \mathrm{mL}\) of PBS solution \((\mathrm{pH}\,7.4)\) and stored at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for further use.
Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Au}@\mathbf{NiFe}\) MOFs. Primarily, the NiFe-based nanoporous MOFs were prepared through a coprecipitation method.\(^{23}\) Typically, nickel chloride hexahydrate \((0.6\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and tri-sodium citrate dehydrate \((0.9\ \mathrm{mmol})\) were dissolved in \(20\ \mathrm{mL}\) of
deionized water, and 0.4 mmol of potassium ferricyanide was dissolved in \(20~\mathrm{mL}\) of deionized water. After that, the above two solutions were mixed together under continuous magnetic stirring followed by aging at room temperature for \(10~\mathrm{h}\). The earthy-yellow precipitate was collected by centrifugation and washed with deionized water and ethanol for two times, respectively. The obtained product was dried overnight at \(50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
Au@NiFe MOFs were obtained by in-situ reduction of gold nanoparticles on the surface of NiFe-based nanoporous MOFs.\(^{17}\) Typically, \(50~\mathrm{mg}\) of the prepared NiFe-based nanoporous MOFs and \(3~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(2\%\) \(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}\) were dispersed in \(20~\mathrm{mL}\) of deionized water under magnetic stirring for \(5~\mathrm{min}\). Afterwards, \(7~\mathrm{mg}\) of PVP were added into the solution for another \(5~\mathrm{min}\) of magnetic stirring to prevent the agglomeration of gold nanoparticles. Then \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(5~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) sodium citrate solution and a drop of sodium borohydride solution were added to the above solution as reductant and continuing stirring for \(12~\mathrm{h}\). The generated atropurpureus precipitation was washed with deionized water until the supernatant was colorless dried overnight at \(50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
Preparation of \(\mathbf{Ab}_{2}\)-Au@NiFe MOFs Bioconjugate. The preparation of \(\mathrm{Ab}_{2}\)-Au@NiFe MOFs bioconjugate was demonstrated in Scheme 1B. Firstly, \(2~\mathrm{mg}\) of Au@NiFe MOFs was dispersed in \(500~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(\mathrm{pH}~7.4\) PBS solution and mixed with EDC (20 mmol) and NHS (10 mmol) for \(2~\mathrm{h}\) at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). After removing the excess crosslinker, the mixture mixed with \(500~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(1~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}~\mathrm{Ab}_{2}\) under vibration at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\). The generated \(\mathrm{Ab}_{2}\)-Au@NiFe MOFs complex was gathered through centrifugation and washed with \(\mathrm{pH}~7.4\) PBS solution for once. After that, \(150~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(1\%\) BSA was mixed
with the above product and vibrated at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(6\,\mathrm{h}\) to block the non-specific active sites on the surface of Au@NiFe MOFs. The final product was rinsed with pH 7.4 PBS solution to wash away the unreacted BSA and dispersed in 1 mL pH 7.4 PBS solution (pH 7.4). Then it was stored at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for further use.
Fabrication of the Dual Quenching ECL Immunosensor. The fabrication of the ECL immunosensor was shown in Scheme 1C. Prior to modification, the electrode (GCE) was polished to a mirror by using 1.0, 0.3, and \(0.05\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) alumina slurry, followed by sonication in deionized water and ethanol. The electrode was rinsed thoroughly with deionized water and dried in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flow. Afterwards, \(6\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(0.8\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\;\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}-\) oxalate MOFs/Ab1 bioconjugate was directly immobilized onto the clear electrode surface, drying at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Subsequently, \(3\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(1\%\) BSA was dropped onto the surface of modified electrode and incubated at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{h}\) to block the non-specific active sites. Next, the superfluous BSA was washed away when the GCE surface was damp and dried. Then \(5\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of different concentrations of \(\mathrm{A}\,\upbeta\) was modified onto the above electrode, incubating at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{h}\) to specific bind with \(\mathrm{Ab}_{1}\). Subsequently, the unreacted \(\mathrm{A}\,\upbeta\) was scoured off when the electrode surface was slightly wet and dried at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Eventually, \(6\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(0.5\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\,\mathrm{Ab}_{2}-\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\,\mathrm{MOFs}\) bioconjugate was incubated onto the prepared electrode through specific binding and rinsed with pH 7.4 PBS solution thoroughly to remove the redundant antibody. The dual quenching MOFs ECL system has been constructed and stored at \(4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for further use.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Morphology Characterization of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs, NiFe-based MOFs
and Au@NiFe MOFs. Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}-\) oxalate MOFs were depicted with different scales in Figure 1A and showed typical octahedral morphology. Besides, the energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) spectrum of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs was obtained in Figure S1 and it could be observed that the major elements were Zn, Ru, C, and O, indicating that the \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) luminophor was encapsulated in Zn-MOFs successfully. Figure 1B and 1C were SEM and HRTEM images of NiFe-based MOFs, respectively, and it could be observed that the NiFe-based MOFs presented as homogeneous cubes with average diameter of 100 nm. As the auxiliary quench agent and the active sites bound with antibodies, Au nanoparticles were evenly distributed on the surface of NiFe-based MOFs by the method of in situ reduction of growth as Figure 1D and 1E shown. In addition, the acquired EDAX mapping images were shown in Figure 1F, which showed that the elements of Ni, Fe were primary components of NiFe-based MOFs and Au nanoparticles were reduced to the surface of NiFe-based MOFs successfully.
Moreover, the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns, the fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) spectrogram and the EDS were acquired to further explore the characters of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs, NiFe-based MOFs, and Au@NiFe MOFs. As shown in figure 2A, the PXRD patterns demonstrated that \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs exhibited well crystallinity and identified to the simulated pattern of the reported single crystal structure. \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs could bind with antibodies through the cross-linking agents since there are plentiful carboxyl groups on the surface of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs as
characterized in Figure 2B. The FT-IR absorption peak at \(1322~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was the C-O vibration of the single bond. The characteristic absorption peak of \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) double bond vibratory expansion appeared at \(1625~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). In addition, the characteristic absorption peak of O-H stretching vibration was discovered at \(3415~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\).<sup>13,17,34</sup> Figure 2C showed the PXRD spectrum of NiFe-based MOFs and Au@NiFe MOFs. It could be observed that the synthesized NiFe-based MOFs exhibited typical diffraction peaks of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}\) (JCPDS card 33-0665) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.93}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.056}\) (JCPDS card 44-1088).<sup>23</sup> And it could also indicated the presence of carboxyl groups over the surface of Au@NiFe MOFs as Figure S2 shown, which could make the Au@NiFe MOFs link with more \(\mathrm{Ab}_{2}\) to improve the sensitivity of the dual quenching ECL immunosensor. Moreover, the crystallinity did not change when the Au nanoparticles were grown in situ on the surface of NiFe-based MOFs. The major elements of Au@NiFe MOFs have been analyzed by EDS spectrum and atomic percentages as Figure 2D shown. Ni, Fe, Au, C, and N were found and used for the constitution of Au@NiFe MOFs as the main elements.
The surface chemical compositions and elemental valence states of the NiFe-based MOFs material were assessed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements. The survey spectrum in Figure S2 obviously indicated the presence of Ni, Fe, and C, which were consistent with the elemental mapping images. For NiFe-based MOFs, the high-resolution spectra of Fe 2p, Ni 2p, and C 1s were presented in Figure 3. In the Ni 2p region (Figure 3A), the binding energies appeared at \(856.3~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(873.9~\mathrm{eV}\), revealing the formation of oxidized nickel species. The high-resolution spectrum of Fe 2p (Figure 3B) displayed two peaks located at \(709.7~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(723.5~\mathrm{eV}\),
confirming that the presence of the oxidation state of iron with \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) as the primary existence form.\(^{23}\) Moreover, the high-resolution spectrum of C 1s (Figure 3C) exhibited one peak seated at \(284.6~\mathrm{eV}\).
Mechanism Investigation of the Dual Quenching MOFs System. \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) luminophore which was inseted in the Zn-oxalate MOFs displayed anodic emission light in the presence of co-reactant tripropylamine (TPA) at the voltage range from 0 to 1.3 V. Two kinds of speculations of the reaction mechanism were supposed in this \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\)-TPA ECL system.\(^{11,35,36}\) Route 1 showed one supposed ECL mechanism to generate excited-state \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\), primarily TPA was directly oxidized to TPA\(^{+}\) in the low potential ECL and deprotonated to TPA. The TPA· radical species reduced \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) to form \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{+}\), which further reacted with the oxidative species TPA\(^{+}\) to form \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\). Route 2 showed another ECL mechanism occurred at high potential. Firstly, \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) was oxidized to \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{3+}\) directly on the electrode, which further reacted with the TPA· radical species to generate \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\). In addition, an alternative mechanism for generating \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\) at the higher potential was raised as that \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{3+}\) generated in Route 2 reacted with \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{+}\) generated in Route 1 to form \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}\).
