TPA - e \(\longrightarrow\) TPA·+ (1a) TPA·+ - H+ \(\longrightarrow\) TPA· (1b) TPA·+ Ru(bpy)32+ Ru(bpy)3+ + product (1c) \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{+}+\mathrm{TPA}^{+}\longrightarrow\mathrm{\Delta~Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+*}+\mathrm{product}\) (1d)
Ru(bpy)32+ Ru(bpy)32+ + hv (1e) Route 2 \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}-\mathrm{e}\longrightarrow\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{3+}\) (2a) TPA – e TPA.+ (2b) TPA·+ – H+ TPA· (2c) \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{3+}+\mathrm{TPA}\cdot\longrightarrow\mathrm{~\mathrm{~Ru}(b p y)_{3}{}^{2+*}~}\) (2d) \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+*}\longrightarrow\mathrm{\Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}+h\nu\) (2e)
To investigate the quenching effect of the \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs by NiFe-based MOFs and Au nanoparticles, the UV-vis absorption, fluorescence emission, and ECL behavior of NiFe-based MOFs, Au@NiFe MOFs and \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs were explored. As shown in Figure 4A, the UV-vis absorption spectrum of NiFe-based MOFs overlapped appropriately with the ECL emission spectrum of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs, which triggered the quenching effect of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs ECL because of the RET between \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs (donor) and NiFe-based MOFs (acceptor). After in situ reduction of Au nanoparticles on the surface of NiFe-based MOFs, the Au@NiFe MOFs compound exhibited a new UV-vis absorption peak at \(350\ \mathrm{nm}\), which increased the spectrum overlap area of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs and Au@NiFe MOFs and achieved a second quenching effect of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) inlaid in Zn-oxalate MOFs. As a result, the quenching efficiency of Au@NiFe MOFs for \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs was higher than NiFe-based MOFs alone.
Figure 4B was the fluorescence emission spectra of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs
(a), the mixture of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs and NiFe-based MOFs (b), and the mixture of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs and \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs (c), respectively. It could be observed that the fluorescence emission intensity of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs decreased when mixed with NiFe-based MOFs, and further decreased when mixed with \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs. Therefore, it could be concluded that NiFe-based MOFs could quench the emitting intensity of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs, while the \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs compound could dual quench of the emitting intensity of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs.
Moreover, the ECL behaviors of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs and the quenchers also were investigated. As shown in Figure 4C, the ECL signal response of the individual \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs was 14200 a.u., and the ECL intensity was reduced to almost half (7150 a.u.) when NiFe-based MOFs was dropped onto the surface of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs/GCE. Meanwhile, when \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs was dropped onto the surface of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs/GCE, the ECL response would be further reduced (6000 a.u.) compared with the curve b. Hence, the dual quenching effect of \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs on \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs ECL was confirmed again.
Characterization of the Immunosensor. In order to demonstrate the modification process of each step for the electrode, the change of the ECL intensity of the proposed immunosensor was shown in Figure 5A. It could be observed that bare electrode has almost no ECL response in the presence of coreactant TPA. Whereas there was a very strong ECL response when \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs were modified onto the
electrode (curve b). And the ECL intensity declined successively when dropped \(\mathrm{Ab}_{1}\), BSA, and \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\) onto the modified electrode, respectively. This was because \(\mathrm{Ab}_{1}\), BSA, and \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\) all were protein and could obstruct electron transfer seriously. The ECL intensity reduced to minimum after dispensed \(\mathrm{Ab}_{2}\)-Au@NiFe MOFs onto the modified electrode finally, which was because that the RET reaction occurred between \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs (donor) and \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs (acceptor) and caused the dual quenching effect on \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) ECL.
It also has been proven that electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was an useful way for the fabrication characterization of the immunosensor. As Figure 5B shown, the electron transfer resistance \(\mathrm{(R_{et})}\) of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs/GCE (curve b) increased compared with that of the bare GCE (curve a), which indicated the successfully assembling of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs. When \(\mathrm{Ab}_{1}\) was joined with the Zn-oxalate MOFs through \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) and -COOH by crosslinking agent (EDS \(40~\mathrm{mg}\), NHC \(10~\mathrm{mg}\)), the value of \(\mathrm{R_{et}}\) increased significantly. After BSA, \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\), and \(\mathrm{Ab}_{2}\)-Au@NiFe MOFs were modified onto the above electrode, the value of Ret continued increasing. This may be due that BSA, \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\), and \(\mathrm{Ab}_{2}\)-Au@NiFe MOFs all contained protein which would hinder the electron transfer greatly.
Optimization of the Detection Conditions. The suitable environment of \(\mathrm{pH}\) was crucial for the performance of the suggested immunosensor. After investigated the adaptability of the immunosensor to the peracidity environment, parlkaline environment, and neutral environment, the neutral environment (pH 7.4) was selected for the following study as shown in Figure 6A. TPA was the coreactant of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\)
encapsulated in the Zn-oxalate MOFs and the concentration of tripropylamine was optimized (Figure 6B). It could be observed that the ECL response achieved the strongest when the concentration of tripropylamine was \(10\;\mathrm{mmol}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\).
In addition, although \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs displayed extraordinary ECL performance, too much of it would trigger the behavior of self-absorption which would shrinkage the ECL response.\(^{40}\) As shown in Figure 6C, the ECL signal increased when the concentration of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs was less than \(0.5\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) and decreased when the concentration was greater than \(0.5\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\). Therefore, \(0.5\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs was used in the ECL system. The quantity of \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs which as the acceptor of RET was also explored as shown in Figure 6D. The ECL intensity decreased to the lowest when the concentration of \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs labeled on the \(\mathrm{Ab}_{2}\) was \(0.8\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\), indicating that the RET achieved the highest efficiency. As a result, \(0.8\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) of \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs was chosen for further study. Performance of the ECL Immunosensor in \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\) Detection. Under the optimal experimental conditions, the analytical performance for the proposed immunosensor was investigated by incubating various concentrations of \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\). The results were presented in Figure 7A. Bare GCE has hardly any ECL response (curve a) and an exceptionally strong ECL signal was obtained when modified \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs onto the bare GCE (curve k). The ECL intensity declined in turn with the increasing concentration of \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\) incubated onto the modified electrode (curve j \(\rightarrow\) b). Moreover, the ECL response of the proposed immunosensor exhibited an excellent linear relationship with the logarithm of \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\) concentration as shown in Figure 7B. The
equation of linear regression was \( I = 5588.26 - 1188.20 \, \mathrm{lg} \, c \) (\( I \) represented the ECL intensity and \( c \) represented the concentration of \( \mathrm{A}\beta \)) and the correlation coefficient was 0.993. The estimated limit of detection (LOD) was \( 13.8 \, \mathrm{fg} \, \mathrm{mL}^{-1} \) (the calculation was shown in the supporting information),<sup>41</sup> which exhibited better performance than that of many other reports as Table S1 shown, indicating the outstanding potentiality of the suggested immunosensor in bioassays.
Furthermore, reproducibility, selectivity, and stability were also important indicators for assessing the analytical performance of a immunosensor.<sup>42</sup> Under the optimized conditions, seven immunosensors were constructed in the same environment to determine \( \mathrm{A}\beta \) simultaneously as shown in Figure 7C (RSD = 1.32%), indicating the superior reproducibility of the proposed immunosensor. Besides, in order to investigate the selectivity,<sup>43</sup> the constructed immunosensor was used to determine the solution of human immunoglobulin (IgG), carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA), insulin, alpha fetoprotein (AFP), \( \mathrm{A}\beta \) oligomers (AβO), \( \mathrm{A}\beta \) fibrils (AβF), \( \mathrm{A}\beta \) and mixture of them, respectively and the results were shown in Figure 7D. It could be observed that the ECL intensity was almost same as that of blank when the detecting system only contained the interferents of IgG, CEA, insulin, AFP, AβO and AβF respectively and significantly decreased when the detecting system contained \( \mathrm{A}\beta \), revealing that the suggested immunosensor was bound to \( \mathrm{A}\beta \) specifically (three groups were implemented simultaneously). The stability was investigated by detecting different concentrations of \( \mathrm{A}\beta \) for three circles and the consequences have been shown in Figure S3., indicating the favourable stability of the proposed immunosensor. Hence, all the excellent
performances could prop up that the constructed immunosensor has potential application in clinical analysis.
Real Sample Analysis. In order to verify the clinical application of the proposed immunosensor, we applied it to determine the concentration of \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\) in the artificial cerebrospinal and testified the accuracy with standard addition method. The results were shown in Table S2. When the additive amount of \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\) was \(0.005~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}\), \(0.1~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}\), \(5~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}\), and \(25~\mathrm{ng~mL^{-1}}\) respectively, the relative standard deviation (RSD) was less than \(5\%\) and the recovery was within the scope of \(99\% \sim 100.76\%\), illustrating the advantageous potential possibility of clinical application.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, a dual quenching ECL immunosensor has been proposed for the ultrasensitive detection of \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\), which based \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}\) encapsulated in Zn-oxalate MOFs \((\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs) as luminophor and \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs as the dual-quencher. The as-prepared \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs with porous structure could decrease the ECL intensity of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs compound due that both Au nanoparticles and NiFe-based MOFs could quench the ECL efficiency as the ECL acceptor in the RET process, which could significantly improve the sensitivity of the immunosensor. Besides, the dual quenching effect was confirmed by the overlap between the UV-vis absorption spectrum of \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NiFe}\) MOFs and the ECL emission spectrum of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs compound. Furthermore, the suggested dual quenching ECL immunosensor exhibited outstanding selectivity, stability, and reproducibility, demonstrating a promising quenching strategy to determinate the \(\mathrm{A}\upbeta\).
It was worth pointing out that the successful use of \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}{}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\)-oxalate MOFs and NiFe-based MOFs in this ECL system also provided the inspiration for other applications of MOFs materials for the researchers in ECL methodology.
